- How would you determine policy precedence when multiple policies apply with conflicting results?
- How would you test hot-reload paths without impacting live requests?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to apply the Command pattern with a runtime registry for runtime extensibility, decoupling command invocation from implementation.

## Key Concepts
- Command pattern for encapsulating each request as an object
- Runtime extensibility through dynamic registration
- Isolation and error handling for fault tolerance
- Async execution for non-blocking operations

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Command {
  name: string;
  execute(args: unknown): Promise<void>;
}

type CommandFactory = () => Command;

class CommandRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, CommandFactory>();

  register(name: string, factory: CommandFactory): void {
    this.map.set(name, factory);
  }

  async execute(name: string, args: unknown): Promise<void> {
    const factory = this.map.get(name);
    if (!factory) throw new Error(`Unknown command: ${name}`);
    const command = factory();
    try {
      await command.execute(args);
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(`Command '${name}' failed:`, err);
      process.exit(1);
    }
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new CommandRegistry();
registry.register('init', () => new InitCommand());
registry.register('build', () => new BuildCommand());
registry.register('deploy', () => new DeployCommand());
await registry.execute('build', { target: 'prod' });
```

## Design Pattern
The **Command pattern** is ideal here because it encapsulates each operation as an executable object, enabling delayed execution, queuing, and undo functionality if needed.

## Trade-offs
- **Pros**: Loose coupling, runtime extensibility, easy testing (mock commands)
- **Cons**: Additional indirection, slightly more complex codebase, registry can become a global state hotspot

